# =============================================================================
# slatwall-ts - environment variable contract
# =============================================================================
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
#   A committed TEMPLATE. It is the single authoritative list of every
#   environment variable this service reads. It holds NO credentials and must
#   never hold any: every value below is either blank or a non-sensitive
#   default that is safe in source control.
#
# HOW IT IS USED
#   Local and test runs
#     Copy this file to `slatwall-ts/.env` and fill in the blanks. That `.env`
#     must never be committed, and the subtree now protects that with a
#     COMMITTED mechanism rather than with instructions: `slatwall-ts/.gitignore`
#     ignores `.env` and `.env.*` and re-includes this template through
#     `!.env.example`, so the contract stays tracked while a real credential
#     cannot be staged. It is committed deliberately - a per-clone
#     `.git/info/exclude` never travels, so a fresh clone would carry no rules
#     at all and a `.env` holding DB_PASSWORD would be one `git add` away from
#     the index. `git status --porcelain` remains the final verification gate
#     before staging, because an ignore rule protects the paths it names and
#     nothing else - see README.md, "Connections, and what not to commit", and
#     the header of `slatwall-ts/.gitignore`, which records why that file exists.
#
#     WHO ACTUALLY LOADS THAT FILE, WHICH IS NARROWER THAN "dotenv is a
#     devDependency" suggests. `dotenv` 17.4.2 is a devDependency, and its ONE
#     importer in the whole subtree is `tests/setup.ts`, which `vitest.config.ts`
#     wires in as a setup file. So:
#       * UNDER TEST, `.env` is loaded automatically. Nothing else is needed.
#       * A LOCAL NON-TEST RUN LOADS NOTHING BY ITSELF. No module under `src/**`
#         imports `dotenv`, and `src/lib/config.ts` - the only reader of
#         `process.env` - reads whatever the process was given and refuses when a
#         required value is absent. Invoking a handler locally therefore needs the
#         variables put into the environment explicitly, by either:
#             set -a; . ./.env; set +a        # export them into the shell
#             node --env-file=.env <script>   # Node 20 loads the file itself
#         Both are equivalent as far as this contract is concerned; neither adds a
#         dependency, and the second needs no shell state at all.
#   Deployed runs
#     The AWS Lambda `nodejs20.x` runtime injects environment variables
#     natively, so no file is read and `dotenv` never executes there.
#
#   READ by `src/lib/config.ts` and by nothing else under `src/**` - eighteen of
#   the nineteen keys below. `src/repositories/mysql/connection.ts`,
#   `src/repositories/mysql/dialect.ts`, `src/lib/logger.ts` and
#   `src/handlers/bootstrap.ts` CONSUME the resolved values without touching
#   `process.env` themselves. The single test-only key at the bottom,
#   TEST_LIVE_DATABASE, is the one exception: it describes the test harness rather
#   than the service, and `tests/setup.ts` reads it.
#
# WHERE THIS CONTRACT COMES FROM
#   The legacy CFML host published its datasource configuration as application
#   values, and FOUR of the keys below descend directly from them. The rest do
#   NOT: this contract is larger than the legacy one, because replacing an
#   external datasource registry and an in-engine ORM with a driver moves
#   decisions into the contract that the CFML host never held. Of the nineteen
#   keys documented here, four replace a legacy value one-for-one (DB_NAME,
#   DB_USER, DB_PASSWORD, DB_DIALECT), one is a protocol default the legacy read
#   from its datasource alias (DB_PORT), one names the server the alias used to
#   name (DB_HOST), and the remaining thirteen - the whole TLS group, the four
#   pool knobs, NODE_ENV, LOG_LEVEL, FEED_ALLOWED_HOSTS, the two ECB keys and the
#   test flag - replace NOTHING and each says so at its own group heading.
#   Calling the file a one-for-one replacement of the four values below would
#   misdescribe two thirds of it.
#
#     Application.cfc:L78-L87
#       L78 -> "datasource"          the datasource name
#       L81 -> "datasourceUsername"  the connecting account
#       L84 -> "datasourcePassword"  that account's credential
#       L87 -> "databaseType"        the ORM dialect
#
#     config/configApplication.cfm:L1-L2
#       L2 fixes the datasource name to the literal `Slatwall`.
#
#     config/configORM.cfm:L1-L15
#       L2-L8 probed the server for its product name and L9-L14 mapped that
#       name onto an ORM dialect. This file replaces the probe with explicit
#       configuration; see the dialect group below.
#
#   Application.cfc:L75 also published `slatwallRootURL` from the FW/1
#   `variables.framework.baseURL`. That is a routing artifact rather than part
#   of the datasource contract, and API Gateway plus `src/handlers/router.ts`
#   replace it, so no base-URL variable appears here.
#
# SCHEMA CONTINUITY
#   This service is built to read and write the EXISTING `Sw*` MySQL tables,
#   unchanged. Every variable below only describes how to reach a database that
#   already exists. Nothing here creates, renames, drops or alters any table or
#   column, and there is deliberately no schema-management switch of any kind.
#
#   WHAT ISSUES THOSE STATEMENTS, so the tense above is read as the capability it
#   is: the subtree carries the connection pool
#   (`src/repositories/mysql/connection.ts`), the dialect resolver
#   (`src/repositories/mysql/dialect.ts`), five extracted SQL modules under
#   `src/repositories/mysql/sql/` and all six MySQL repository adapters. Every
#   variable below is consumed on a live path: `config.ts` validates it at
#   startup, `connection.ts` turns it into a pool, and the adapters read and
#   write `Sw*` rows through that pool.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database connection
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Legacy origin: Application.cfc:L78-L87 and config/configApplication.cfm:L1-L2.
#
# Read by `src/lib/config.ts` and turned into a pool by
# `src/repositories/mysql/connection.ts`.
#
# A missing or invalid value here is a HARD STARTUP ERROR, never a degraded
# mode. The legacy host behaved the same way: config/configORM.cfm:L4-L7 caught
# a failed datasource probe, rendered `admin/views/main/nodatasource.cfm` and
# then aborted the request outright. Failing loudly and immediately is the
# preserved behavior, not an addition.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Host name of the MySQL server holding the `Sw*` schema.
# REQUIRED. Intentionally blank: no host name, network address or connection
# string is committed to this repository, not even for local use.
DB_HOST=

# TCP port of that server.
# The registered default for MySQL is 3306. This is a protocol default rather
# than a credential, so it is supplied as a real default.
DB_PORT=3306

# Schema (database) name.
# Default is the literal `Slatwall`, verbatim from
# config/configApplication.cfm:L2, which sets this.datasource.name to
# "Slatwall". The capital S is significant: MySQL schema names are
# case-sensitive wherever the server stores them case-sensitively, and the
# `Sw*` tables live under this exact name.
DB_NAME=Slatwall

# Account used to connect. Mirrors `datasourceUsername` (Application.cfc:L81).
# REQUIRED. Intentionally blank; supply it per environment.
#
# GRANT IT DATA ACCESS AND NOTHING ELSE. The service issues only SELECT, INSERT,
# UPDATE and DELETE, and it issues them only against the `Sw*` tables of the one
# schema `DB_NAME` names. It never issues DDL and never issues DCL: it creates
# no table, alters no column, adds no index and grants no privilege, because AAP
# 0.8.1 admits no schema change at all. So the account this variable names does
# not need - and should not hold - CREATE, ALTER, DROP, INDEX, REFERENCES,
# TRIGGER, CREATE ROUTINE, GRANT OPTION, FILE, PROCESS, RELOAD or SUPER. The
# minimum that works, verified by running every published route under exactly
# it, is:
#
#   CREATE USER 'slatwall_app'@'%' IDENTIFIED BY '<supplied at deploy time>';
#   GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON `Slatwall`.* TO 'slatwall_app'@'%';
#
# ⚠ ALL FOUR VERBS ARE REQUIRED, INCLUDING FOR THE PARTS THAT ONLY READ. The
# sku-code guard in `src/repositories/mysql/mysqlSkuRepository.ts` serializes
# concurrent writers with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`, and MySQL 8 refuses a locking
# read to an account holding SELECT alone - it answers `ERROR 1142 ... SELECT
# with locking clause command denied`. A read-only grant therefore cannot serve
# the mutating routes, and narrowing to SELECT is not a safe economy.
#
# The subscription tables the price-group path reaches through
# (`SwSubsUsageBenefitAccount`, `SwSubsUsageBenefit`,
# `SwSubsUsageBenefitPriceGroup`, `SwSubsUsage`, `SwSubscriptionStatus`,
# `SwType` - see AAP 0.2.2's one deliberate data-layer exception) are read only,
# and they live in the same schema, so the grant above already covers them and
# no second grant is needed.
DB_USER=

# Credential for that account. Mirrors `datasourcePassword`
# (Application.cfc:L84).
# REQUIRED, and it must stay blank in this template. Not even a placeholder
# value belongs here, because a placeholder in a committed file is
# indistinguishable from a real leak during review. Provide the value at run
# time through the process environment or a managed secret store.
DB_PASSWORD=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database transport security (TLS)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resolved by `src/lib/config.ts` and applied by
# `src/repositories/mysql/connection.ts` as the driver's `ssl` options.
#
# WHY THIS GROUP EXISTS AND HAS NO LEGACY ORIGIN
#   Unlike every other variable in this file, this group replaces nothing. The
#   legacy host did not configure transport at all: the credentials read at
#   Application.cfc:L81 and L84 were blank, so the CFML engine fell back to the
#   datasource ALIAS registered in the ColdFusion administrator, and whether that
#   alias negotiated TLS was a property of the server administration - outside
#   this repository entirely. Replacing an external datasource registry with a
#   driver means the transport decision moves INTO the contract, because there is
#   no administrator left to make it. Omitting the group does not preserve legacy
#   behavior; it silently chooses plaintext.
#
#   The MySQL protocol authenticates the CLIENT to the server. Without TLS
#   nothing authenticates the SERVER to the client and nothing protects the
#   session, so an on-path actor can observe or alter credentials, statements and
#   commerce data. That is why this group is REQUIRED rather than optional.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How the connection to the MySQL server is protected.
#
# REQUIRED, WITH NO DEFAULT - deliberately, and for the same reason DB_DIALECT
# has none: a transport posture that is assumed rather than stated is a posture
# nobody reviewed. There is no "preferred" or "opportunistic" value on purpose,
# because a mode that silently downgrades to plaintext when the handshake fails
# is indistinguishable from no protection at all.
#
# Accepted values:
#
#   verify-identity   TLS with full verification: the server's certificate chain
#                     is verified against the trusted authority AND the host name
#                     presented in the certificate must match DB_HOST. THE
#                     RECOMMENDED VALUE, and the only one that defeats an on-path
#                     actor who can present a validly-signed certificate for a
#                     different host.
#                     REQUIRES DB_HOST TO BE A NAME, and this is now ENFORCED at
#                     startup rather than described: an IP literal - dotted quad,
#                     bracketed IPv6 or bare IPv6 - is REJECTED, because a
#                     certificate binds to names and the mode would otherwise
#                     reduce silently to a chain-only check, which is not what its
#                     name promises. To reach a database by address, use
#                     `verify-ca` with a pinned DB_TLS_CA.
#
#   verify-ca         TLS with certificate-chain verification but no host-name
#                     check. Use ONLY when identity cannot be checked for a
#                     structural reason - DB_HOST is an IP literal, or the
#                     connection terminates at a proxy whose certificate names a
#                     different host. Strictly weaker than `verify-identity`.
#                     REQUIRES DB_TLS_CA. Because this mode omits the host-name
#                     check, the trust anchor is the ONLY thing binding the
#                     connection to the intended server; trusting the built-in
#                     public root store instead would accept ANY certificate
#                     signed by ANY public authority, which an on-path actor can
#                     obtain for a domain it controls. Leaving DB_TLS_CA blank
#                     here is therefore a hard startup error.
#
#   disabled          No TLS. Permitted ONLY when DB_HOST is a loopback address,
#                     and REJECTED as a hard startup error otherwise - in every
#                     environment, including `development`.
#
# THE LOOPBACK RULE IS ENFORCED, NOT ADVISORY, AND IT IS THE RULE THAT CARRIES THE
# GUARANTEE. `src/lib/config.ts` refuses `disabled` unless DB_HOST is one of:
#
#   127.0.0.1, or any address in 127.0.0.0/8
#   ::1  (or the bracketed [::1], or the written-out 0:0:0:0:0:0:0:1)
#   localhost
#
# A name that merely RESOLVES to a loopback address is NOT accepted. That module
# performs no name resolution - it has no imports at all, so that it can raise its
# own startup failure before anything else has loaded - and whether a deployment
# starts on plaintext transport must not depend on a resolver's answer. Spell the
# literal.
#
# PRODUCTION FAILS CLOSED TOO, ON TOP OF THE HOST RULE. When NODE_ENV is
# `production`, `disabled` is REJECTED whatever the host - the same posture
# config/configORM.cfm:L4-L7 took toward an unusable datasource, which rendered a
# diagnostic and then `<cfabort/>`ed rather than continuing in a degraded mode.
#
# WHY BOTH RULES EXIST, when the second looks like it implies the first. It does not,
# and the gap it leaves is real: NODE_ENV DEFAULTS to `development` (see its own
# entry below), so an environment that simply OMITTED it never reached the
# production check - and a deployment could send the account, the credential and
# every statement to a REMOTE server in cleartext while startup reported nothing.
# The host rule closes that by making the condition the host rather than a variable
# that has a permissive default; the production rule remains as the stricter
# posture on top of it.
#
# CERTIFICATE VERIFICATION IS NEVER DISABLED. There is no variable here that
# turns it off, and none may be added: both TLS modes above pin the driver's
# `rejectUnauthorized` to true. If a handshake fails, fix the trust anchor with
# DB_TLS_CA below - do not look for a switch to silence it, because that switch
# is exactly the vulnerability this group closes.
DB_TLS_MODE=

# The lowest TLS protocol version this service will negotiate.
#
# OPTIONAL, defaulting to TLSv1.2.
# Accepted values: TLSv1.2 | TLSv1.3   (matched without regard to case)
#
# This is a FLOOR, not a selection: the handshake still negotiates the highest
# version both ends support, and this value only rules out anything below it. Set
# TLSv1.3 when every server the deployment talks to supports it, which forbids
# 1.2 outright.
#
# THE ACCEPTED SET IS CLOSED, AND THAT IS THE POINT. TLS 1.0 and 1.1 are
# deprecated and are simply not accepted values - `src/lib/config.ts` types this
# as a two-member union, so a deployment cannot lower the floor below 1.2 by any
# spelling, and a typo is a hard startup error rather than a silent downgrade.
# A mode of `verify-identity` says the certificate was verified; it says nothing
# about the strength of the protocol carrying it, which is why this is a separate
# setting rather than something the mode implies.
#
# Ignored entirely when DB_TLS_MODE is `disabled`, where nothing is negotiated. A
# malformed value is still reported in that case rather than hidden.
DB_TLS_MIN_VERSION=

# The certificate authority to trust, as an inline PEM bundle.
#
# CONDITIONALLY REQUIRED - and which condition applies is the whole of it:
#
#   with verify-identity   OPTIONAL. Leave it blank and the runtime's built-in
#                          public root store is trusted, which is correct for a
#                          managed database whose certificate a public authority
#                          signed, because the HOST-NAME CHECK is what binds that
#                          certificate to the intended server.
#
#   with verify-ca         REQUIRED. This mode is defined by omitting the
#                          host-name check, so nothing else binds the certificate
#                          to the server and the anchor cannot be optional.
#                          Leaving it blank is a hard startup error.
#
#   with disabled          IGNORED. There is no handshake for an anchor to
#                          participate in. A leftover value is not rejected, so
#                          switching a development machine to `disabled` stays a
#                          one-variable edit.
#
# Set it to the PEM text of your authority - one or more
# `-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----` blocks - to trust THAT authority instead of the
# public root store, which is what a private or provider-specific certificate
# authority requires.
#
# A certificate authority certificate is public by definition, so this is not a
# credential and a real value may be committed to a deployment's own environment
# configuration. It still does not belong in THIS file, which is a template.
#
# The value is the PEM TEXT ITSELF, never a file path. `src/lib/config.ts`
# performs no filesystem access at all - it has no imports - and the deployed
# runtime injects environment variables natively, so a path would have nothing to
# resolve against. Literal two-character `\n` sequences are accepted and
# converted to real newlines, so the bundle also survives a transport that
# cannot carry a multi-line value.
#
# IT IS BOUNDED AT 2048 BYTES, WHICH IS ONE CERTIFICATE AND NOT A CHAIN. See the
# DELIVERY-SIZE CONTRACT at the foot of this file: the whole environment has to fit
# in AWS Lambda's 4096-byte quota, and this is the largest of the nineteen
# variables. If your authority does not fit, do not raise the number - one of these
# will be true instead:
#
#   * the certificate is signed by a PUBLIC authority, in which case leave this
#     blank and the runtime's built-in root store already trusts it;
#   * you are supplying a full CHAIN where only the trust ANCHOR is needed - supply
#     just the authority that signs the server's certificate;
#   * the deployment genuinely needs a large private bundle, which is a case the
#     environment cannot carry at all and which has to be delivered another way.
DB_TLS_CA=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database dialect
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Legacy origin: config/configORM.cfm:L1-L15. Resolved by
# `src/repositories/mysql/dialect.ts`.
#
# The legacy host never configured the dialect - it PROBED for it. L3 asked the
# datasource for its version information and L9-L14 mapped the reported
# DATABASE_PRODUCTNAME onto an ORM dialect using `findNoCase`, i.e.
# case-insensitive SUBSTRING matching:
#
#     product name contains "MySQL"     ->  MySQL
#     product name contains "Microsoft" ->  MicrosoftSQLServer
#     product name contains "Oracle"    ->  Oracle10g
#
# This port replaces that runtime probe with explicit configuration, so the
# value is stated rather than discovered.
#
# REQUIRED, WITH NO DEFAULT - by design, and the reasoning has to be stated
# precisely because the legacy behaviour is easy to overstate. The conditional
# chain ends at config/configORM.cfm:L15 with a bare closing tag and NO
# `<cfelse>`, so an unrecognized product name left the dialect UNSET - and
# EXECUTION CONTINUED. The `<cfabort/>` on L6 guards only the datasource probe's
# `<cfcatch>` (L2-L8); it has nothing to do with the mapping below it. So the
# legacy did not fail closed on an unrecognized product; it carried on with no
# dialect at all.
#
# WHAT IS PRESERVED IS THE ABSENCE OF A GUESS, not a refusal. No default appears
# here for the same reason no `<cfelse>` appeared there: neither implementation
# will invent a dialect it was not given.
#
# THE HARD STARTUP ERROR IS A DELIBERATE IMPROVEMENT ON THE LEGACY, AND IS LABELLED
# AS ONE. `src/repositories/mysql/dialect.ts` refuses an unset or unrecognized
# value outright rather than continuing unset, because an ORM that silently
# defaulted its dialect and a driver that must interpolate a dialect-specific SQL
# fragment fail very differently: the former produced wrong SQL somewhere later,
# the latter would produce it immediately and at a site that decides money.
# Calling that refusal "preserved legacy behaviour" would be wrong, so it is not
# called that.
#
# Accepted values, exact casing as written by config/configORM.cfm:L10, L12
# and L14:
#
#     MySQL | MicrosoftSQLServer | Oracle10g
#
# Only the MySQL branch is implemented by this port. `dialect.ts` still
# recognizes all three literals - so that a misspelling is distinguishable from
# a valid but unimplemented choice - and rejects `MicrosoftSQLServer` and
# `Oracle10g` at startup with an explicit error. The variable stays
# parameterized rather than being hardcoded because THREE live in-scope SQL
# sites branch on the dialect - the same three `dialect.ts` encodes, re-counted
# against the legacy source rather than carried over from the specification,
# which listed only the first two:
#
#     model/dao/PromotionDAO.cfc:L482-L488   the productTypeIDPath concatenation
#     model/dao/PriceGroupDAO.cfc:L57        the row-limiting clause
#     model/dao/SkuDAO.cfc:L194-L198         the option-group positional weight
#                                            in getSortedProductSkusID
#
# Engine requirement for the MySQL branch: THIS PROJECT TARGETS MySQL 8.0 OR
# NEWER. That is a project constraint carried from the Agent Action Plan, and it
# is deliberately NOT attributed to the driver: the `mysql2` 3.23.1 package
# pinned in package.json documents broad MySQL compatibility and states no such
# server minimum of its own.
DB_DIALECT=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Connection pool
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `src/repositories/mysql/connection.ts` creates exactly ONE pool at module
# scope, so a warm Lambda container reuses its connections across invocations
# instead of opening a new connection per invocation. That is an engineering
# decision established by direct experiment during planning.
#
# The four knobs below are operational configuration for that pool and map
# one-to-one onto `mysql2` pool options. They are conservative starting points
# to be tuned per environment. They are NOT targets of any kind: the legacy
# system defines no such target, and none is invented here.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Maximum simultaneous connections the pool will open (`connectionLimit`).
DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT=10

# How long to wait for a TCP connection to be established, in milliseconds
# (`connectTimeout`).
DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# Maximum idle connections the pool retains (`maxIdle`). Keeping this at or
# below DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT is what lets a warm container hold connections
# open between invocations.
DB_MAX_IDLE=10

# How long an idle connection is retained before the pool closes it, in
# milliseconds (`idleTimeout`).
DB_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=60000


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Standard Node environment selector, read by `src/lib/config.ts` and by nothing
# else under `src/**`.
# `vitest` sets this to `test` on its own, so it need not be set for `npm test`.
# Accepted values: development | test | production
#
# IT DOES EXACTLY TWO THINGS, and no third thing.
#
#   1. `production` REFUSES `DB_TLS_MODE=disabled` outright, as a hard startup
#      error. See the transport group above. This is the one security-relevant
#      effect, and it fails closed.
#   2. It is reported back as the resolved environment on the loaded
#      configuration, so a caller can say which environment it booted in.
#
# IN PARTICULAR IT DOES NOT GOVERN HOW MUCH OF AN ERROR REACHES A LOG LINE, which is
# worth stating because an environment flag is the usual place such a gate lives.
# `src/lib/logger.ts` emits an error's `message` and `stack` in NO environment: it
# projects every error to its class name plus a stable code such as
# `ER_ACCESS_DENIED_ERROR`, and substitutes a redaction marker for both text
# fields, unconditionally.
#
# The reason for removing that gate rather than tightening it: the text is
# dangerous for reasons that have nothing to do with the environment. A driver
# builds its message from what the SERVER sent and attaches the failing statement
# to the same object, so the message can carry `Sw*` table and column names and
# any value the server quoted back; a stack publishes absolute paths and the
# internal call chain. An environment-gated version of that disclosure is only as
# reliable as the variable, and this variable is exactly the kind of thing that is
# unset in a container, misspelled in a template, or inherited from a developer's
# shell. Never emitting the text needs no variable to be correct, so there is no
# configuration in which the safe behaviour can be lost.
NODE_ENV=development


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Logging
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `src/lib/logger.ts` writes structured JSON to stdout, which Lambda captures
# natively. No logging library is used - that was a deliberate rejection - so
# this level is the only control over WHICH entries are emitted.
#
# IT IS READ BY `src/lib/config.ts`, NOT BY THE LOGGER. That module reads NO
# environment variable of its own; the threshold is resolved and validated with
# every other key and handed over by `src/handlers/bootstrap.ts` while it wires
# the composition root. The two files cannot import one another - configuration
# must be able to fail before logging exists, and logging must be able to report
# that failure - so the composition root is the seam between them.
#
# THE VALIDATION IS DELIBERATELY LENIENT, AND IT IS THE ONLY KEY HERE THAT IS. An
# unset, blank or unrecognized value falls back to `info` and does NOT fail the
# process: a threshold able to abort a cold start would leave a misconfigured
# service unable to explain itself. The coercion is not silent either - it is
# announced once per container. What is NOT announced is the value that was
# rejected: it is discarded the moment it fails to match, and is never echoed
# into a log line or retained anywhere, because the shape of a mistyped level name
# is also the shape of an access key or a short token pasted into the wrong
# variable.
#
# HOW MUCH OF AN ERROR APPEARS INSIDE AN EMITTED ENTRY IS NOT CONFIGURABLE AT ALL.
# An error's `message` and `stack` are replaced with a redaction marker
# unconditionally, in every environment and at every level - `debug` shows no more
# of them than `error` does, and NODE_ENV above changes nothing about it. What an
# entry carries instead is the error's class name plus a stable code such as
# `ER_ACCESS_DENIED_ERROR`. See the NODE_ENV entry above for why that gate was
# removed rather than tightened.
#
# So this level answers one question - which entries are worth emitting - and no
# setting in this file, alone or in combination, will make a log line carry an
# error's text.
# Accepted values: error | warn | info | debug
LOG_LEVEL=info


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Product feed
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by `src/lib/config.ts` as `AppConfig.feed.allowedHosts`, and consumed by
# `src/handlers/bootstrap.ts` when it constructs the product-feed port.
#
# THE HOSTS THIS DEPLOYMENT MAY PUBLISH A GOOGLE PRODUCT FEED FOR. A comma
# separated list of bare host authorities - host only, optionally with a port. No
# scheme, no credentials, no path, no query, no fragment. Matching is
# case-insensitive and surrounding whitespace is ignored, so the list may be
# written however is readable.
#
#   FEED_ALLOWED_HOSTS=shop.example.com
#   FEED_ALLOWED_HOSTS=shop.example.com,eu.shop.example.com,localhost:3000
#   FEED_ALLOWED_HOSTS=feed_internal.example.com,[2001:db8::1]:8443
#
# THE ACCEPTED SHAPE IS DECIDED BY ONE PARSER, SHARED WITH THE RENDERER. A host is
# one or more dot-separated labels, each starting and ending alphanumeric with
# hyphens and underscores permitted between, OR a bracketed IPv6 literal; an entry
# may be at most 259 characters (RFC 1035's 253 plus a port); and a port, when
# written, must be 1 to 65535 WITH NO LEADING ZERO - `:0`, `:65536`, `:99999` and
# `:08443` are all refused. `src/integrations/google/rssFeedRenderer.ts` validates
# the host it publishes through the SAME function, so a value this list accepts
# cannot be refused at render time and a value it refuses cannot be authorized here.
# Two separate patterns would be free to disagree about uppercase, underscores, IPv6
# literals and the port range; one shared parser cannot.
#
# ***** REQUIRED TO SERVE THE FEED. UNSET MEANS NO FEED IS PUBLISHED. *****
#
# WHY THIS IS CONFIGURATION AND NOT A REQUEST FIELD. The legacy template rendered
# whatever authority the request arrived on - the literal `http://#CGI.HTTP_HOST#`
# appears at five sites in
# `integrationServices/google/views/feed/product.cfm` - so the host in a merchant
# feed was request-derived. This port still OBSERVES the host on the request, and the
# request is what SELECTS which authorized host is served; what this variable does is
# DEFINE THE AUTHORIZED SET, and MEMBERSHIP IS MANDATORY. The list has to live in
# process configuration for the check to mean anything: a caller that supplied both
# the candidate and the allow-list could allow itself, which makes the check vacuous.
#
# WHY THE LEGACY'S "HOST FROM THE REQUEST" IS NOT REPRODUCED AS-IS. CFML read
# CGI.HTTP_HOST from a request that had already reached a web server bound to the
# hostnames the deployment owns, so the deployment constrained that value before the
# application ever saw it. An API Gateway proxy event carries whatever Host a client
# writes. Reading it without a list therefore does not reproduce the legacy's
# provenance - it lets a caller choose the authority written into every link of a
# merchant feed that Google then fetches and follows. This list is where the
# deployment states the authorities it owns.
#
# TWO STATES, AND BOTH FAIL CLOSED ON AN UNLISTED HOST:
#
#   UNSET, or set to a value naming no host  (indistinguishable, and how this file
#   ships - the declaration below is LIVE and empty)
#     NO authority is authorized, so NO FEED IS PUBLISHED. Every request to
#     GET /feeds/google/products is refused with `UntrustedFeedHostError`, and the
#     log line names this variable so the fix is actionable. Nothing else in the
#     service is affected: the other four capabilities do not read this key, and a
#     deployment that does not serve a feed needs no value here.
#
#   SET TO ONE OR MORE HOSTS
#     Only those authorities are served, and REQUEST HOST DECIDES WHICH ONE. The
#     renderer publishes the normalized request host once membership is proved; the
#     list is process configuration, so no request can write the set it is checked
#     against. Required for any deployment that publishes the feed.
#
# WHY THE KEY SHIPS LIVE AND EMPTY RATHER THAN COMMENTED OUT. Unset and empty resolve
# to the SAME empty list, so commenting it out buys nothing behaviourally and costs an
# operator the chance to notice the key at all. Every other key in this contract ships
# live; this one is not an exception. The fail-closed state is stated as a REQUIREMENT
# at the top of this section rather than as a default that happens to refuse, so that
# serving the feed reads as one variable to set instead of as an empty document to
# diagnose.
#
# A malformed entry is a START-UP FAILURE, not a silently dropped list member: a
# deployment that wrote `https://shop.example.com` here meant to publish a feed
# and would otherwise be left refusing every request with no indication why.
FEED_ALLOWED_HOSTS=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Product-feed URL scheme - THERE IS NO SUCH VARIABLE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTHING TO SET. This section exists because `FEED_URL_SCHEME` is a key an operator
# would reasonably expect to find, and its ABSENCE IS DELIBERATE rather than an
# oversight - so the reason is written here instead of being inferred from silence.
#
# The scheme is not a deployment choice: the legacy template
# hardcodes the literal `http://` at all five URL sites in
# `integrationServices/google/views/feed/product.cfm` (L14, L15, L22, L23, L24),
# and the agent action plan freezes the Google product-feed integration contract
# exactly (0.1.1, 0.8.1), permitting only three named divergences in this port
# (0.6.7) - of which this is not one. `src/integrations/google/rssFeedRenderer.ts`
# owns the literal as `FEED_ORIGIN_SCHEME_PREFIX` and carries the full reasoning.
#
# A DEPLOYMENT THAT MUST PUBLISH `https` FEED URLS CANNOT GET THEM FROM THIS
# CONTRACT, AND TERMINATING TLS IN FRONT OF THE SERVICE DOES NOT CHANGE THAT.
# Stated plainly because the distinction is easy to lose: a load balancer or CDN
# in front of this service governs how a CLIENT reaches it, and rewrites nothing
# inside a generated document. The scheme in every feed URL is a literal emitted
# by `rssFeedRenderer.ts`, so a fronted deployment still publishes `http://`
# origins in its feed body unless something downstream rewrites them. Only the
# origin's HOST half is configurable here, through FEED_ALLOWED_HOSTS above.
#
# NOTHING IN THE DB_TLS_* GROUP BEARS ON THIS EITHER. That group protects THIS
# service's connection to MySQL. It is not feed transport, it does not reach the
# renderer, and no value in it can alter a URL in an emitted document.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Currency conversion rates
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by `src/lib/config.ts` as `AppConfig.currency`, and consumed by
# `src/handlers/bootstrap.ts` when it constructs the currency converter.
#
# THE EUROPEAN CENTRAL BANK REFERENCE RATES USED TO CONVERT A SKU PRICE INTO A
# CURRENCY IT HAS NO STORED PRICE FOR. Step 3 of the SKU currency cascade
# (`model/entity/Sku.cfc:L416-L428`) reaches this path: where no base price and no
# per-currency override exists, the price is converted on the fly.
#
#   ECB_REFERENCE_RATES=USD=1.0850,GBP=0.8520,JPY=163.41
#   ECB_RATES_RETRIEVED_AT=2026-08-04T00:00:00Z
#
# Each entry reads CODE=RATE, where CODE is exactly three letters and RATE is the
# number of units of that currency per ONE EURO. Entry order is irrelevant and
# surrounding whitespace is ignored.
#
# DO NOT LIST `EUR` ITSELF. The euro is the pivot both halves of a conversion go
# through, so it is implicit: a rate is divided by to reach euros and multiplied by
# to leave them, and the euro needs neither. Listing it is harmless but describes
# nothing.
#
# WHY THIS IS CONFIGURATION AND NOT A FETCH. The legacy service downloaded these
# rates itself, over plain HTTP, from `http://www.ecb.int/...`
# (`model/service/CurrencyService.cfc:L109`), memoized them, and refetched once a
# day. That fetch is NOT ported: the pinned dependency set carries no HTTP client
# and no XML parser, both deliberately, so the daily refresh belongs to whatever
# publishes this value rather than to the service reading it. Without this variable
# there would be no supply route at all and every deployment would run with an empty
# table, so this is where the rates enter.
#
# THREE INTEGRITY CHECKS, ALL AT START-UP. A malformed entry, a rate that is not a
# plain positive decimal, or a missing retrieval instant is a START-UP FAILURE
# rather than a silently dropped list member - a bad rate does not misprice one
# order, it misprices every order until someone notices.
#   1. SHAPE. Three letters, `=`, then a plain decimal numeral. No sign and no
#      exponent: `1.085e0` is refused, because a rate written that way is far more
#      likely a mistake than an intention.
#   2. STRICTLY POSITIVE. Zero is refused explicitly, because conversion DIVIDES by
#      the source currency's rate. A negative rate cannot be written at all - the
#      numeral shape admits no sign.
#   3. THE INSTANT. `ECB_RATES_RETRIEVED_AT` is REQUIRED whenever any rate is
#      supplied - rates of unknown age cannot be reported as stale, which is the
#      whole point of collecting it - and setting the instant WITHOUT any rates is
#      also refused, since it then describes nothing. Three properties are checked,
#      each closing a way a value could look fine and mean something else:
#
#      a. IT MUST BE A COMPLETE ISO-8601 INSTANT WITH AN EXPLICIT ZONE, either `Z`
#         or an offset such as `+02:00`:
#
#             2026-08-04T00:00:00Z          accepted
#             2026-08-04T02:00:00+02:00     accepted (the same instant)
#             2026-08-04T00:00:00           REFUSED - no zone
#             2026-08-04                    REFUSED - date only
#             Aug 4 2026                    REFUSED - not ISO-8601
#
#         "Parsable" is NOT the standard, and the difference is not pedantry: every
#         refused form above is accepted by JavaScript's own date parser, and the
#         zoneless one is interpreted in the HOST'S LOCAL TIME - so the same value
#         would denote a different instant on two machines, which is precisely what
#         this port's explicit UTC policy exists to prevent. Once a zone is present
#         no conversion is needed: the value denotes one absolute instant.
#      b. THE DATE MUST EXIST. `2026-02-30T00:00:00Z` is refused rather than rolled
#         forward to 2 March, which is what the date parser does with it silently.
#      c. IT MUST NOT BE IN THE FUTURE, beyond a five-minute allowance for
#         unsynchronized clocks. A future instant makes the table's age NEGATIVE,
#         and a negative age is less than any refresh window - so every staleness
#         check reads it as fresh, and the one value whose purpose is to expose a
#         stale table would instead guarantee it looked current.
#
# STALENESS IS REPORTED, NOT ENFORCED. On start-up the age is compared against the
# one-day window the legacy service refreshed on, and a table older than that is
# logged as a warning - a count and an age, never a rate or a code. It is still
# USED. That is deliberate and matches the legacy behaviour precisely: its refetch
# sat in a `try` whose `catch` was empty, so a failed download left the previous
# table in place and it went on serving rates of any age. Refusing a stale table
# here would diverge in the WORSE direction, because the alternative to a day-old
# rate is not a correct rate - it is no conversion at all, which returns the amount
# unchanged and prices a foreign-currency SKU as though the two currencies were at
# parity. Cents of drift versus tens of percent.
#
# UNSET OR BLANK MEANS A CONVERSION THAT NEEDS A QUOTE PASSES THE AMOUNT THROUGH
# UNCONVERTED. It is not refused, and nothing raises. `convertCurrency` is a TOTAL
# function in this port: whenever a quote cannot be produced - no table configured
# at all, or a table that does not quote one of the two codes - the amount is
# returned exactly as it was received, the gap is reported to the operator on the
# log stream once per resolution, and no error reaches the caller. Configure both
# variables if you sell in more than one currency; read the paragraph below before
# deciding that the pass-through is acceptable for your catalog.
#
# THE TWO LEGACY FAILURE STATES, AND WHICH ONE THIS PORT REPRODUCES:
#   * TABLE PRESENT, ONE CODE UNLISTED. `model/service/CurrencyService.cfc:L100-L101`
#     returns the amount UNCONVERTED. This is must-preserve behaviour and it is
#     preserved exactly.
#   * NO TABLE AT ALL. The daily fetch sits in a `try` whose `catch` is empty, and the
#     very next statement returns `variables.europeanCentralBankRates`
#     (`model/service/CurrencyService.cfc:L104-L131`) - a variable that was never
#     assigned on a cold start, which CFML refuses at runtime. The legacy RAISES here.
#     THIS PORT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT, and the reason is blast radius rather than
#     convenience: step 3 of the SKU cascade (`model/entity/Sku.cfc:L416-L428`) calls
#     the converter for every eligible currency that carries no explicit override, so
#     a raise there does not fail one conversion - it fails the whole
#     `getCurrencyDetails()` build, and with it every price that SKU can answer for
#     any currency, including its base one. An unconfigured rate table would become a
#     total pricing outage. So the [L100-L101] pass-through covers both states, the
#     misconfiguration is reported instead of being answered with an exception, and
#     `ECB_RATES_RETRIEVED_AT` is what makes an aging table visible.
#
# WHAT THE PASS-THROUGH COSTS, STATED PLAINLY, BECAUSE IT IS MONEY. An unconverted
# amount is a base-currency numeral published as a foreign-currency price - two
# currencies treated as though they were at parity. That is a bigger error than a
# day-old rate, which is why the paragraph above refuses to withhold a stale table:
# the alternative to a slightly stale quote is no quote at all. An earlier revision
# of this note therefore had the port REFUSING a conversion with no table, on the
# reading that the legacy raises there. A code review reverted it - the adapter was
# throwing where its own port contract promised a total function, and the throw
# landed on the whole cascade - and the total function is the frozen contract. If a
# pass-through would be wrong for your catalog, the answer is to configure the table,
# not to expect the runtime to stop.
#
# A DEPLOYMENT QUOTING A SINGLE CURRENCY CONFIGURES NOTHING HERE AND IS STILL CORRECT.
# No conversion is attempted for it at all: step 3 of the cascade
# (`model/entity/Sku.cfc:L416-L428`) only converts currencies that need converting, and
# the euro pivot converts to itself without a rate. Both variables are optional, but
# neither is optional once the other is set.
ECB_REFERENCE_RATES=
ECB_RATES_RETRIEVED_AT=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test-only
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not read by anything under `src/**`. Read only by `tests/setup.ts`, which
# `vitest.config.ts` wires in as a setup file.
#
# The integration tier under `tests/integration/repositories/` asserts SQL shape
# and parameter binding against a recording executor, so it needs no database
# server at all. Any suite that does require a live server must gate itself on
# this flag - never on a hardcoded connection - so that the default checkout runs
# green with no server present. That guarantee holds across the whole suite as it
# stands: no suite in the tree requires a server, and this variable is read only
# by `tests/setup.ts`.
#
# WHAT THE FLAG IS FOR, then: it is a documented escape hatch for any suite that
# would ever genuinely need a live server, so that such a suite gates on a
# declared variable rather than on a connection written into source, and the
# default checkout stays green with no server present. It is not a licence to
# write one.
#
# `tests/setup.ts` NORMALISES IT AND REFUSES A VALUE IT DOES NOT RECOGNISE.
# Enabling values are `true`, `1`, `yes`; disabling values are `false`, `0`, `no`
# and the empty string; unset means disabled. Anything else stops the run before
# a single test executes, because a typo must not silently skip the very suites it
# was set to enable while the run still reports success. Note the asymmetry with
# production configuration, which is REQUIRED and has no default: this key is
# test-only, optional, and explicitly defaulted.
TEST_LIVE_DATABASE=false


# =============================================================================
# DELIBERATELY ABSENT
# =============================================================================
# Recorded so that an absence can be told from an oversight. Each entry is a variable
# a reader might expect to find here, with the reason it is not part of this contract.
#
#   Cloud provider access keys, session tokens or a region variable
#     Nothing in this slice calls a cloud service, and Lambda supplies its own
#     execution context and region.
#
#   Any Google product-feed credential
#     The adapter is complete - the integration contract, the adapter that
#     implements it, the feed repository, the feed service and the RSS renderer
#     are all present, and a feed document IS produced. It still needs no
#     credential and no endpoint override, because it makes NO live outbound
#     Google call: it reads the `Sw*` schema and returns an RSS 2.0 document as a
#     string. That is the specified behaviour rather than an unfinished state -
#     the integration is a stub with respect to CALLING Google, and complete with
#     respect to GENERATING the feed. Nothing about it is ever configured here.
#     The one feed-related key in this contract is FEED_ALLOWED_HOSTS above,
#     which is an allow-list and not a credential.
#
#   Any schema-management or data-loading switch
#     See SCHEMA CONTINUITY in the header: the `Sw*` tables are used exactly as
#     they already exist.
#
#   Any infrastructure-as-code variable, such as a stack name or a deployment
#   target
#     Infrastructure code is out of scope for this port. "Deployable" here
#     means a build and package step that emits a Lambda-compatible artifact,
#     not a live deployment.
#
#   Any legacy content-bridge variable
#     That bridge is not ported. Its identifier column on the category entity,
#     and the associated `site` reference, survive only as inert persisted
#     columns so the schema contract stays intact - with no behavior attached.
#
#   Any lock-duration variable
#     The legacy 60-second order-placement, 45-second payment-transaction and
#     30-second bean-factory first-scan locks are noted and deliberately not
#     implemented; all three sit in paths this port does not cover.
#
#   Any package-registry token
#     There are no private packages, so there is no registry credential to
#     provision and no `.npmrc` to configure.
#
#   Any base URL for the service itself
#     See the note on Application.cfc:L75 in the header.
#
#   Any switch that relaxes or disables TLS certificate verification
#     There is no `DB_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED`, no `DB_TLS_INSECURE` and no
#     `DB_TLS_ALLOW_SELF_SIGNED`, and none may be added. Both verifying modes
#     pin the driver's `rejectUnauthorized` to true, so a certificate that does
#     not chain to the trusted authority fails the handshake. An untrusted
#     authority is fixed by supplying it through DB_TLS_CA, never by lowering the
#     check: a connection that encrypts without verifying is defeated by any
#     on-path actor able to present a certificate of its own, which makes such a
#     switch strictly worse than the honest `disabled` mode it would be mistaken
#     for.
#
#   Any client-certificate variable
#     There is no `DB_TLS_CERT` or `DB_TLS_KEY`. This service authenticates with
#     the account and credential above; mutual TLS is a different authentication
#     mechanism, and adding half of one would imply a capability that is not
#     implemented. DB_TLS_CA is about which SERVER this client trusts, which is
#     the opposite direction.
#
#   Any switch that re-enables verbose error detail in production
#     There is no `LOG_ERROR_STACKS`, no `LOG_VERBOSE_ERRORS` and no
#     `DEBUG` flag, and none may be added. There is no gate to re-open: an
#     error's message and stack are never emitted in ANY environment, so the
#     safe behaviour is a property of the code rather than of a variable. Adding
#     a switch would let a deployment publish driver text, `Sw*` schema names and
#     internal file paths while still calling itself production - the exact
#     outcome removing the environment gate made unreachable. Diagnose a
#     production failure from the stable code and the correlation identifier on
#     the mapped response.
#
#   Any cipher variable
#     There is no `DB_TLS_CIPHERS`, and none may be added: hand-narrowing the
#     suite list is how a deployment ends up weaker than the platform default,
#     so cipher selection is left to the runtime.
#
#     Note that the protocol FLOOR is configurable - see DB_TLS_MIN_VERSION
#     above - and that this is not the same concession. Its accepted values are a
#     closed two-member set, so the variable can only ever RAISE the floor to TLS
#     1.3; there is no value it accepts that selects anything weaker than the
#     TLSv1.2 default. A cipher list has no such bound, which is why one is
#     configurable and the other is not.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# DELIVERY-SIZE CONTRACT - the whole environment has to fit in 4096 bytes
# =============================================================================
#
# AWS Lambda caps the ENTIRE environment-variable map at 4 KB - 4096 bytes, keys
# and values together - and the quota is NOT adjustable. A function whose
# configuration exceeds it is refused at `UpdateFunctionConfiguration`: the
# deployment never goes out, and no code in this subtree runs to explain why.
#
# Two variables above invite an unbounded value - DB_TLS_CA accepts inline PEM, and
# ECB_REFERENCE_RATES an arbitrarily long rate list - which is how an otherwise
# perfectly valid environment becomes undeployable. Every variable therefore carries a
# documented maximum, and `src/lib/config.ts` refuses both an over-size VALUE and
# an over-budget SET at start-up. That refusal is most useful OUTSIDE Lambda -
# locally and in CI, where the value is authored and a mistake is cheap to fix.
#
# THE MAXIMA, IN UTF-8 BYTES (a non-ASCII character costs two to four):
#
#   DB_HOST                   253    RFC 1035's host limit
#   DB_PORT                    16
#   DB_NAME                    64    MySQL's identifier limit
#   DB_USER                    32    MySQL's account-name limit
#   DB_PASSWORD               128    RDS caps a MySQL master password at 41
#   DB_TLS_MODE                24
#   DB_TLS_MIN_VERSION         16
#   DB_TLS_CA                2048    ONE certificate authority, not a chain
#   DB_DIALECT                 24
#   DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT        16
#   DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS      16
#   DB_MAX_IDLE                16
#   DB_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS         16
#   NODE_ENV                   24
#   LOG_LEVEL                  16    budget allocation only - never refused (see below)
#   FEED_ALLOWED_HOSTS        512    roughly two maximal hosts
#   ECB_REFERENCE_RATES       512    roughly forty rate entries; the ECB publishes ~30
#   ECB_RATES_RETRIEVED_AT     32
#   TEST_LIVE_DATABASE         16    test-only; never present in a deployed function
#
# THE SHORT VALUES ARE BOUNDED GENEROUSLY ON PURPOSE. 16 to 24 bytes is far more
# than the longest accepted spelling of any enumeration or integer above, and that
# is deliberate: this is a DELIVERY bound, and a bound tight enough to catch a typo
# would turn it into a second, worse shape check. `NODE_ENV=developement` is
# refused by the NODE_ENV rule, which names the accepted values; a byte count would
# say nothing useful about it.
#
# LOG_LEVEL IS BUDGETED BUT NEVER INDIVIDUALLY REFUSED. Its figure above is an
# allocation for the arithmetic, not a threshold. An unset or unrecognized LOG_LEVEL
# must not be able to abort a cold start - this service reports its own fatal
# configuration failure THROUGH the logger - so a size refusal is not available for
# that one key. Its bytes still count toward the aggregate, where the question is
# whether the SET can be delivered rather than what the threshold should be.
#
# THE ARITHMETIC. The nineteen key names total 250 bytes and one byte per entry is
# reserved for per-entry overhead, so 269 bytes are spoken for before any value.
# The maxima total 3781, giving a documented ceiling of 4050 bytes against the
# 4096-byte quota - 46 bytes of headroom.
# `tests/traceability/legacyTestMap.ts` recomputes that sum from the source and
# fails the suite if it ever exceeds the quota, so a raised maximum or a twentieth
# variable is caught in CI rather than at deployment.
#
# ONLY THESE NINETEEN ARE MEASURED. The `AWS_*` variables the Lambda runtime
# injects are not part of the function's configured map and cannot be shrunk by a
# deployment; a developer's shell carries hundreds more that have nothing to do
# with this service. Measuring either would make the budget unreachable.
#
# IF A VALUE DOES NOT FIT, THE ANSWER IS NOT A LARGER NUMBER. For a certificate
# authority, see the three alternatives recorded on DB_TLS_CA above. For a rate
# table, publish fewer currencies - only the currencies this deployment actually
# quotes need a rate, and a currency with no rate passes through unconverted
# rather than failing. A configuration that genuinely needs more than 4096 bytes
# cannot be delivered through Lambda environment variables at all, whatever this
# service does, and has to be delivered by another mechanism.
# =============================================================================
